To implement rate limiting and distributed rate limiting mechanism in Node.js with a distributed data store, you can use Redis as the data store and the `ioredis` library to interact with it. Below is an example code that demonstrates how to achieve this:

```javascript
const Redis = require('ioredis');
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

// Create a Redis client
const redis = new Redis({
  host: 'localhost',
  port: 6379,
});

// Rate limit settings
const MAX_REQUESTS_PER_HOUR = 100;
const BLOCK_TIME = 60 * 60; // 1 hour in seconds

// Middleware to handle rate limiting
const rateLimitMiddleware = async (req, res, next) => {
  const ip = req.ip;
  const key = `rate_limit:${ip}`;

  // Check if IP address is blocked
  const blocked = await redis.get(`block:${ip}`);
  if (blocked) {
    const remainingTime = await redis.ttl(`block:${ip}`);
    return res.status(429).send(`Too Many Requests. Try again in ${remainingTime} seconds.`);
  }

  // Check if IP address has exceeded the rate limit
  const currentRequests = await redis.incr(key);
  if (currentRequests === 1) {
    // Set the expiry of the key to 1 hour
    await redis.expire(key, 60 * 60);
  }

  if (currentRequests > MAX_REQUESTS_PER_HOUR) {
    // Block the IP address and set the expiry for the block
    await redis.set(`block:${ip}`, 'true', 'EX', BLOCK_TIME);
    return res.status(429).send('Too Many Requests. Please try again later.');
  }

  next();
};

// Webhook endpoint with rate limiting
app.post('/webhook', rateLimitMiddleware, (req, res) => {
  // Process the webhook request
  res.send('Webhook processed successfully!');
});

// Start the server
app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server listening on port 3000');
});
```

In the above code, the `rateLimitMiddleware` function acts as a middleware that is executed for every incoming request to the `/webhook` endpoint. It first checks if the IP address is blocked, and if so, returns a 429 error response. Then, it increments the request count for the IP address stored in Redis. If the request count exceeds the rate limit, the IP address is blocked and a 429 error response is returned.

To implement distributed rate limiting, you can deploy multiple instances of this Node.js server and configure them to connect to the same Redis instance. As Redis is a distributed data store, all instances will be able to access and update the request count and blocked IP addresses consistently.

Additionally, the blocked IP addresses are automatically cleared after the specified block time has elapsed. Redis's `EX` option is used while setting the block key to automatically expire it after the specified time.

