Write a JavaScript function to check if a string contains the given substring in a case-sensitive manner, but the function should not use built-in string methods like includes() or indexOf().

The function should have a time complexity of O(n), where n is the length of the string.

**Reference Erroneous Code:**
```javascript
function checkSubstring(string, substring) {
  let index = 0;

  while (index < string.length) {
    if (substring[index] !== string[index]) {
      return false;
    }
    index++;
  }

  return true;
}
```

The given code is incorrect as it compares each character of the substring with the corresponding character of the string. Instead, we need to check if the substring exists anywhere within the string, not just at the beginning. 

To fix the code, we need to iterate over each character of the string and compare it with the first character of the substring. If they match, we check if the remaining characters of the string starting from that index match the remaining characters of the substring. 

Here's the corrected code:

```javascript
function checkSubstring(string, substring) {
  let index = 0;
  let subIndex = 0;

  while (index < string.length) {
    if (string[index] === substring[subIndex]) {
      subIndex++;

      if (subIndex === substring.length) {
        return true;
      }
    } else {
      subIndex = 0;
    }

    index++;
  }

  return false;
}
```

